# III. The hidden algorithm behind the 112 methods

The methods look diverse:

* breath;
* mantra;
* bodily centres;
* sound;
* space;
* darkness;
* pleasure;
* shock;
* fear;
* desire;
* sleep;
* perception;
* imagination;
* conceptual gaps;
* the sense of “I.”

But nearly all follow one abstract algorithm.

Let ordinary experience be:

[
D_i(t)
======

\operatorname{Organize}
\left(
M_i,
C_i(t),
SM_i(t),
W_i(t)
\right),
]

where:

* (M_i): manifest presence;
* (C_i): current contents;
* (SM_i): self-model;
* (W_i): structured world.

Normally attention follows:

[
C_1
\rightarrow
C_2
\rightarrow
C_3.
]

The text inserts a perturbation:

[
\mathcal P_k(D_i)
]

that weakens the current organization of:

* object;
* location;
* temporal continuation;
* conceptual support;
* subject–object separation.

Then:

[
\operatorname{Organization}_i
\downarrow
]

while:

[
M_i
]

remains.

The intended realization is:

[
\boxed{
\text{content can dissolve or reorganize}
}
]

without:

[
\boxed{
\text{manifestness itself disappearing}.
}
]

This produces the core contemplative inference:

[
\boxed{
M_i
\neq
C_i.
}
]

Experience is not identical with any specific object, thought, breath phase, sensation, emotion, bodily location, or world representation.

