# IV. The master variable is the junction

The most important word in the text’s practical logic is not “concentration.”

It is:

[
\boxed{
\textbf{the middle, interval, junction, pause, or transition.}
}
]

The practices repeatedly direct awareness toward the point where one stable organization has ended but the next has not yet formed.

Examples include:

* between inhalation and exhalation;
* between exhalation and inhalation;
* before a sound arises;
* after a sound disappears;
* between two thoughts;
* after one object is dropped and before another is selected;
* between waking and sleep;
* between desire and satisfaction;
* during sudden surprise;
* where bodily space seems to open into surrounding space.

The abstract structure is:

[
S_a
\rightarrow
J_{a,b}
\rightarrow
S_b,
]

where (S_a) and (S_b) are organized states and (J_{a,b}) is the junction.

Ordinary cognition passes rapidly through:

[
J_{a,b}.
]

The method expands it:

[
\operatorname{Gain}(J_{a,b})\uparrow.
]

The tradition claims that the junction reveals Bhairava because it temporarily suspends the determinate structures by which awareness appears exclusively as:

[
\text{this subject knowing this object}.
]

This is the unifying discovery behind a large fraction of the 112 techniques.

