# V. Breath: the organism’s visible oscillation

The first major cluster begins with respiration.

Dyczkowski translates the early breath verses as directing awareness toward the two “voids” in which breath rests at the inner and outer turning points. When awareness does not immediately follow the returning movement, Bhairava is said to become manifest through Bhairavī.

The important structure is not simply breath retention.

It is:

[
\text{outward flow}
\rightarrow
\text{pause}
\rightarrow
\text{inward flow}
\rightarrow
\text{pause}.
]

Respiration supplies a naturally recurring oscillation linking:

* autonomic regulation;
* bodily sensation;
* attention;
* temporal prediction;
* arousal;
* self-location.

The method exploits its phase boundaries:

[
\phi_{\mathrm{exhale}}
\rightarrow
J_{\mathrm{outer}}
\rightarrow
\phi_{\mathrm{inhale}},
]

[
\phi_{\mathrm{inhale}}
\rightarrow
J_{\mathrm{inner}}
\rightarrow
\phi_{\mathrm{exhale}}.
]

At the turning point:

* the expected movement temporarily ceases;
* the motor prediction reverses;
* bodily time loses its normal directional cue;
* attention can remain without pursuing an object.

In CTPI language, breath practice perturbs:

[
\tau_i
+
SM_i^{\mathrm{body}}
+
\operatorname{PolicyPrediction}_i.
]

In QRI terms, it may entrain global oscillatory phase and alter the stability of the phenomenal field.

The traditional claim is stronger:

[
\boxed{
\text{the still centre of the oscillation reveals}
}
]

[
\boxed{
\text{the motionless manifest ground of its movement}.
}
]

This should not be reduced to stopping the breath. Forceful retention is neither required by the philosophical point nor safe as unsupervised experimentation.

